WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.368
This episode is sponsored by Manukora Honey

00:00:08.042 --> 00:00:16.129
In May 2013, a shipment of around 1.5 tons
of cheese was refused entry into the United States.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:21.949
The cheese in question was a spherical, orange
variety known as mimolette that’s sometimes

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:25.713
compared to cantaloupes in looks, and lemons
in taste.

00:00:25.713 --> 00:00:28.265
There’s nothing super startling about
that description—

00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:32.160
nothing to suggest, at least, that this is
a suspicious cheese.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.637
But according to the Food and Drug Administration,
this shipment of mimolette had a problem:

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:38.868
it had mites.

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:57.957
If the idea of mites on your 
cheese makes your skin crawl,

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:00.280
we have good and bad news for you.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.790
The good news is that most cheeses are not
usually filled with so many mites that the

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.250
FDA decides to take action.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.598
The bad news?

00:01:09.598 --> 00:01:15.310
Well…this mite was scraped from the rind
of a Milbenkase, a cheese from Germany that

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.899
is also known for its lemon-y flavor.

00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:22.020
And if you like milbenkase and mimolette but
hate mites…

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.557
the bad news is, you’ve been eating
a lot of mites.

00:01:26.917 --> 00:01:30.750
The footage we’re showing you today was
sampled and recorded by Chloe Savard, one

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.310
of our guest masters of microscopes.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:37.220
The last time we featured her videos, we were
looking at the hidden structures behind

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.554
our favorite fruits and vegetables.

00:01:39.554 --> 00:01:42.493
This time, she brought us cheese.

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:46.083
Like right now, you are looking at the rind
of a milbenkase.

00:01:46.083 --> 00:01:49.750
There’s a rough sort of graininess to the
texture, and you can imagine that it might

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.210
feel bumpy beneath your fingers.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.627
But you might also notice that some of those
bumps are moving.

00:01:56.627 --> 00:02:02.236
And that feeling under your fingertips is now
probably a lot less pleasant to imagine.

00:02:02.236 --> 00:02:06.530
And that sensation might become worse when you
imagine putting some of that cheese into your

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:10.672
mouth, knowing that some of those mites will
find their way into your body.

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:13.461
Now as gross as it is to imagine, 
and maybe it isn't.

00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:16.835
It's just eating animals which lots
of people do all the time.

00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:18.933
I cannot blame the mites,

00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:21.513
Cheese is delicious!

00:02:21.513 --> 00:02:25.381
Except that the mites aren't actually here 
because the cheese tastes good.

00:02:25.381 --> 00:02:26.724
It’s really more the reverse:

00:02:26.724 --> 00:02:30.701
the cheese tastes good to us because the mites
are there.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.880
Milbenkase is the cheese that it is because
of those mites.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.650
It begins as a low-fat curd cheese, kind of
like cottage cheese, mixed with salt and herbs,

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.369
and then it is ripened in a wooden box.

00:02:45.369 --> 00:02:51.630
Inside of that box are cheese mites, more specifically
the species Tyrolichus casei.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:57.250
These mites feed on rye flour that is added
to the box, producing digestive juices full

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:02.247
of enzymes that help carry out the fermentation
needed to develop the cheese.

00:03:02.247 --> 00:03:06.890
And after anywhere from a month to a year,
you can eat the cheese, mites and all.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:11.208
It’s estimated that there are around 2000
mites in each square centimeter of the rind.

00:03:11.208 --> 00:03:15.129
Mimolette cheese is also made with the help
of these mites, though the species and the

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:17.269
processes are a little different.

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:21.909
The mites of mimolette eat the crust of the
cheese and leave behind tiny holes that help

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:23.302
the cheese breathe.

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:28.269
The mite also excretes compounds that impact
the ripening of the cheese…

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:30.823
which is another not so pleasant image.

00:03:30.823 --> 00:03:36.519
Despite their differences, milbenkase and
mimolette both owe their flavor to the mites.

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:41.640
Scientists have traced the lemony taste of
the cheese directly to a compound called neral,

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.610
which is found in lemon oil…

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.661
and in cheese mite secretions.

00:03:46.661 --> 00:03:51.167
Remove the cheese mites from the process,
and you take away that flavor.

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:55.837
In the case of mimolette, though, the mites
are brushed off the cheese before shipment.

00:03:55.837 --> 00:04:01.349
And yet enough do remain that sometimes, they
can reproduce into a population that is large

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:04.394
enough to alarm the FDA.

00:04:04.394 --> 00:04:09.319
According to news reports in 2013, the FDA
didn’t have an official limit on how many

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:11.195
mites could be present on cheese.

00:04:11.195 --> 00:04:15.730
But ideally, they wanted there to be no more
than 1 mite per square centimeter.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:20.780
And the shipment that was making its way into
the US had more mites than that, so the FDA

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.049
stepped in.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:26.100
For us casual cheese eaters, that intervention
might seem reasonable.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.730
Mites are pests.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:32.970
We see them again and again in the microcosmos,
and they often turn up in places that we do not

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.434
want them and they cause various 
conditions that are not comfortable.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:39.367
And in some people who spend a long time
working with these cheeses,

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.903
mites can cause allergies.

00:04:41.903 --> 00:04:44.347
But for the people who love the cheese,

00:04:44.347 --> 00:04:47.780
 the crackdown on mimolette 
was not well-received.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:51.873
This is a cheese that has been around for
centuries without much issue.

00:04:51.873 --> 00:04:57.130
Plus, let’s face it, cheese often includes
some strange ingredients, plucked straight

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.498
from the invisible world of the microcosmos.

00:05:05.339 --> 00:05:07.511
Like let’s take a moment with this clip.

00:05:07.511 --> 00:05:10.399
Just looking at it, where do you think it
came from?

00:05:10.399 --> 00:05:11.960
This is not a trick question or anything.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:16.677
If you saw the blue, and you immediately thought
“blue cheese,” well, you are correct.

00:05:16.677 --> 00:05:21.150
These samples were taken from the blue veins
of the cheese, which are actually dug out

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.267
by a fungus called Penicillium roqueforti.

00:05:24.267 --> 00:05:29.030
These molds used to be found naturally in
milk and cheese, and people would even take

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:34.929
spores from the best cheese of the last batch
and inoculate it on bread to use again.

00:05:34.929 --> 00:05:39.134
But today, spores are often added during the
cheese-making process.

00:05:39.134 --> 00:05:44.910
As the fungus grows, it breaks down fats in
the cheese and produces new flavors

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:48.519
The good news is that it is also safe for
consumption.

00:05:48.519 --> 00:05:53.430
As is cheese made with Geotrichum candidum,
a fungus used in the production of a number

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.896
of soft cheeses, where it grows on the surface
of the rind.

00:05:56.896 --> 00:06:01.210
In fact, it is so widely used in French cheese
that one estimate suggested that a French

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:09.190
person will eat around 8 kilograms of cheese
containing Geotrichum candidum in just one year.

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:14.030
Geotrichum candidum lives in a variety of
habitats, including soil and grass...

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:19.079
but also in raw milk, which is why it’s often
found in raw milk cheeses.

00:06:19.079 --> 00:06:24.770
And with enzymes that break down protein and
fat, the fungus can shape the aroma and taste

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:26.496
of the cheese overall.

00:06:26.496 --> 00:06:30.992
In some, it even leave behind a velvety
surface to the cheese.

00:06:30.992 --> 00:06:35.741
The overall effect of the fungus, though,
depends on the strain of Geotrichum candidum.

00:06:35.741 --> 00:06:40.020
Some create what’s known as “peau de crapaud,”
or “toad skin.”

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:44.023
And some are known to impart a greasy or bitter
quality.

00:06:44.023 --> 00:06:48.264
And there are times when perhaps those traits
are exactly what you want.

00:06:48.264 --> 00:06:56.771
In a way, it’s like art made from the microcosmos,
a careful adjustment to suit a person’s taste.

00:06:56.771 --> 00:07:02.461
And maybe that is what made the response
to the FDA’s mimolette blockage so strong.

00:07:02.461 --> 00:07:05.029
On the one hand, it’s cheese.

00:07:05.029 --> 00:07:09.490
There are plenty of cheeses available at the
store, and to those of us who are perhaps

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:14.690
of less discerning taste, it seems like there
should be plenty of alternatives should your

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:17.865
favorite cheese be held up at the border.

00:07:17.865 --> 00:07:23.825
But art is art, even if some of the artists
are members of the microcosmos.

00:07:23.825 --> 00:07:29.960
They weave blue veins or construct velvety
rinds or add hints of lemony brightness to

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:35.900
the flavor, and through centuries of traditions,
we have been the wielders and consumers of

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:40.069
an art they know purely as survival.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:43.740
And our own tastes, it seems, have won out.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:48.107
We haven’t found a lot of official information
detailing the FDA’s stance on mimolette today.

00:07:48.107 --> 00:07:54.169
But we did find a 2015 post on a cheese blog
that mentions seeing the cheese return to

00:07:54.169 --> 00:07:55.758
local stores.

00:07:55.758 --> 00:07:58.395
Which is good news for everyone…

00:07:58.395 --> 00:08:02.818
except maybe for the mites that find their
way into your stomach.

